{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"length","index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"kind\":\"final\",\"decision\":\"answer\",\"answer\":754,\"evidence\":[\"receipt-1\"],\"confidence\":0.9,\"status\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"reason\":\"Fibonacci recurrence a_n=a_{n-1}+a_{n-2} with a_1=","reasoning_content":"We need count binary strings length 13 that avoid forbidden substrings \"000\" and \"111\". So no run of 3 same bits. Count sequences of length n with no 3 consecutive equal bits. This is classic recurrence. Let a_n be number of valid strings length n. We can use recurrence: a_n = a_{n-1} + a_{n-2}. Wait that's for no consecutive 3? Let's derive.\n\nWe can model with states: last run length and bit. Let f(n, k, b) where k=1 or 2 length of current run of bit b. For n=1: f(1,1,0)=1, f(1,1,1)=1. For n>1: transition: from state (k,b) we can either continue same bit if k<2: go to (k+1,b). Or switch bit: go to (1,1-b). So recurrence.\n\nWe can compute total a_n = sum over states. Let's compute quickly via recurrence: Let A_n be number of strings length n ending with run length 1 (i.e., last two bits different). Let B_n be number ending with run length 2 (i.e., last two bits same but not 3). Then total a_n = A_n + B_n.\n\nTransitions:\nFrom previous length n-1:\n- To state A_n: we must switch bit from any previous state. So A_n = a_{n-1} (since any string of length n-1 can append opposite bit). Because switching always results in run length 1.\n- To state B_n: we must extend a run of length 1 from previous state. So B_n = A_{n-1} (since only strings ending with run length 1 can extend to length 2). Because if previous run length 2, cannot extend.\n\nThus recurrence:\nA_n = a_{n-1}\nB_n = A_{n-1} = a_{n-2}\nSo a_n = A_n + B_n = a_{n-1} + a_{n-2}.\n\nBase: a_1 = 2 (0,1). a_2: all 4 strings? Check forbidden 000,111 length 2 cannot contain 3. So all 4 valid. So a_2=4.\n\nThus Fibonacci-like: a_1=2, a_2=4. Then a_3 = a_2 + a_1 = 6. Let's verify manually: length3 strings avoiding 000,111. All 8 minus 2 forbidden =>6 correct.\n\nSo compute up to n=13. Let's compute quickly.\n\nn: a_n\n1:2\n2:4\n3:6\n4: a3+a2=6+4=10\n5: a4+a3=10+6=16\n6: 16+10=26\n7: 26+16=42\n8: 42+26=68\n9: 68+42=110\n10: 110+68=178\n11: 178+110=288\n12: 288+178=466\n13: 466+288=754\n\nThus answer 754. Provide final JSON with answer 754, evidence receipt-1, confidence maybe 0.9, status SUPPORTED, reason short."}}],"created":1790946874,"model":"C:\\Users\\d2j3\\Documents\\Codex\\2026-09-10\\napraw\\outputs\\moon-native\\models\\gpt-oss-20b-MXFP4.gguf","system_fingerprint":"b10809-5266f24da","object":"chat.completion","usage":{"completion_tokens":768,"prompt_tokens":4111,"total_tokens":4879,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0}},"id":"chatcmpl-qVPGwryMkR1OpfeqFSzgqlTrgfojUqO4","timings":{"cache_n":0,"prompt_n":4111,"prompt_ms":502.442,"prompt_per_token_ms":0.12221892483580638,"prompt_per_second":8182.038921905414,"predicted_n":768,"predicted_ms":2390.628,"predicted_per_token_ms":3.1168552803129077,"predicted_per_second":320.83619868921465}}